# Intro
A reproduction of Smith et al. 2021 (MNRAS 506, 3882), Figure 2: four
co-located ionizing sources in a uniform 100 cm^-3 background, with a
10 pc-radius, 10^4 cm^-3 clump centered 20 pc away along +x (HEALPix
nside=1 pixel 4's center direction). Each source is 19.2 Msun
(Q_H ~ 2.5e48 s^-1), matching the paper's 4 sources totalling
Q_H ~ 1e49 s^-1 "equivalent to a typical O-type star".

See `../StromgrenSphereClump/` for the sibling example that instead
calibrates clump placement against this code's own measured (not
classical) ionization front reach -- useful when reproducing a specific
paper's absolute scale isn't the goal.

Density values (100 / 1e4 cm^-3), clump geometry (10 pc radius at 20 pc),
gas mass resolution (20 Msun), and the ionizing photon rate per source
come directly from the paper. Defaults: `HII_rebuild_time_Myr=0.01`,
`HII_deterministic_boundary_ionization=0` (probabilistic; validated as
the better default -- see `theory/GEAR/Radiation/`), `initial_metallicity=0`.

The diffuse background and clump are set to different temperatures in
pressure equilibrium (`n*T` equal on both sides) by `makeIC_clump.py`'s
`--temperature` (background 1000 K by default, matching the paper).
`SPH:initial_temperature: 0` in params.yml is required for this --
otherwise it overwrites the per-particle IC values.

## Metallicity
`initial_metallicity=0`, `gas_mass=20` is this example's default and the
configuration to run if you just want to see the code's own behaviour
(`./run.sh`, no overrides). It matches both papers' effective choice
(pure hydrogen; neither algorithm has a metallicity term) at their own
gas resolution. Unlike their flat 1e4 K ionized-gas floor, this code's
floor is metallicity-dependent and reaches ~47,500 K at Z=0, which both
lowers the recombination cost and raises the D-type driving sound speed
-- so Z=0 runs land systematically *above* the papers' reference numbers
(expected, not a bug), and `r_HII` can approach
`Stars:HII_max_search_radius` (45 pc) and the 50 pc box half-width for
several rebuild cadences (see `theory/GEAR/Radiation/`, Section
"Metallicity and the ionized-gas temperature floor" for the numeric
breakdown of the two effects). For a direct apples-to-apples comparison at the papers' own
fixed-1e4K convention -- and to stay well inside those defaults -- run
`initial_metallicity=0.231 ./run.sh` (the Z/Zsun that makes
T_collisional=1e4K exactly): this reproduces their reference closely
without needing a larger box or search radius.

## Running a full matrix
A $2\times2\times3$ sweep (scheme $\times$ metallicity $\times$ rebuild
cadence), at a given `gas_mass`, can be generated with:
```
for nside in 0 1; do
  for rebuild_time_myr in 0.01 0.05 0.1; do
    run_name=gas${gas_mass}_z${z_label}_nside${nside}_rebuild${rebuild_time_myr/./p} \
      nside=$nside rebuild_time_myr=$rebuild_time_myr \
      initial_metallicity=$initial_metallicity gas_mass=$gas_mass ./run.sh
  done
done
```
run once with `gas_mass=20 initial_metallicity=0` (this example's
default) and `gas_mass=20 initial_metallicity=0.231` (paper
convention), and again at `gas_mass=95` (this project's zoom-in
resolution) for both metallicities. `z_label`/`gas_mass` above are just
naming placeholders, not real env vars -- substitute the actual values
by hand or in your own wrapper. Once every cell of the matrix you care
about has been run, see "Measuring clump erosion" below.

## Resolution
Gas particle mass defaults to 20 Msun, matching Smith et al. 2021's own
gas resolution for this test -- roughly 175k particles total. Hu et
al. 2017's own convergence-test levels (Appendix A: 4, 0.5, 0.0625,
0.0078125 Msun) are also available via `gas_mass=X ./run.sh`; particle
count scales as `1/gas_mass`, and the two finest levels are cluster-scale,
not laptop-scale.

`rebuild_time_myr`, `deterministic`, `nside`, `initial_metallicity`, and
`density_factor` are all overridable via env var (see run.sh).
`density_factor=1 ./run.sh` degenerates the clump to the background
density, reproducing the papers' own no-clump reference test directly.

# Configure
```
./configure --disable-mpi --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix
```

```
make -j
```

# Run
`nside=0 ./run.sh` and `nside=1 ./run.sh` (use `run_name=nside0 ./run.sh` / `run_name=nside1 ./run.sh` to keep both outputs) run the geometry from Figure 2, reproducing the top (spherical) and bottom (HealPix) panels respectively.

`run.sh` runs `plot_temperature_hii.py` on the run's own final snapshot
automatically (`run_analysis=1` by default; set `run_analysis=0` to skip
it). It prints `r_HII`/`r_physics` and writes
`temperature_{projection,slice}_<snapshot>.png` next to the run's own
output (inside `$run_name/` if set).

# Measuring clump erosion (ID-tracked)

Run after the full `run_name=gas${gas_mass}_z${z_label}_nside${nside}_rebuild${rebuild_time_myr}`
sweep above (each script globs run directories by that naming convention
in the current directory, so run these once every cell you care about
has actually been run -- not automatically from `run.sh`, since they
need the whole matrix, not just the run that just finished):

```
python3 clump_erosion_matrix.py            # total mass remaining, full matrix, bar chart
python3 clump_hemisphere_erosion.py        # near (star-facing) vs. far split + mass-conservation check
python3 clump_hemisphere_density_change.py # near vs. far median n_H, final/t=0 (continuous, not threshold-based)
```

Radially-binned diagnostics (median n_H vs. distance from the clump
centre) are deliberately not used here: erosion in this test is a
directional, near-hemisphere-only effect (see "Mass-biasing" in
`theory/GEAR/Radiation/`), and averaging over the whole sphere at a
given radius dilutes it with the far side's near-total absence of
change.

All three identify clump-member particles once, by particle ID, at
`snapshot_0000` (position within `clump_radius_pc` of the clump centre,
density above the clump's own IC threshold), then look the same IDs up
at the final snapshot wherever they have since moved to. This is
deliberate: re-selecting by position and density at the final time
instead (an earlier version of this measurement) implicitly assumes the
clump neither drifts nor has non-clump gas swept into its old spatial
footprint. Neither holds in practice -- that approach produced an
internally inconsistent result (a hemisphere showing more than 100% of
its own t=0 mass remaining, impossible for a fixed set of particles
under mass conservation) and is not used here. `clump_hemisphere_erosion.py`'s
"still-present at all" numbers are the direct check that ID-tracking
itself conserves mass: they must read 100% in every configuration
(no gas particle is created, destroyed, or loses its ID), so any
deviation there indicates a bug in the analysis, not real clump
physics.

`clump_erosion_matrix.py`'s "still-dense" (n_H above the clump's own IC
threshold) metric only catches gas pushed below that threshold; it
reads ~100% even when the near side is being compressed rather than
diluted. `clump_hemisphere_density_change.py`'s continuous ratio
catches that case.

All three scripts take `--gas-mass`/`--metallicity`/`--rebuild` to
select a subset of the matrix instead of the full sweep; see `--help`.

## A note on metallicity when comparing against old results
This example's `initial_metallicity=0` default gives this code's
natural, non-isothermal T_i ~ 47,500 K (see "Metallicity" above) -- a
materially hotter ionized phase than the T_i=1e4 K STARBENCH/Hu-Smith
are validated against via
`IONIZATION_FEEDBACK_DEBUG_FIXED_IONIZED_TEMPERATURE_K=1e4`. The two
are not interchangeable for erosion/compression measurements: a
~4.75x hotter ionized phase drives a correspondingly larger pressure
contrast against the clump. Match T_i (build flag or
`initial_metallicity=0.231`) before comparing erosion numbers across
runs, or the comparison is confounded by two different physical
regimes, not the same physics at two points in time.

Verified directly (not just inferred from the build flag): with
`IONIZATION_FEEDBACK_DEBUG_FIXED_IONIZED_TEMPERATURE_K=1e4`, the
ionized core's measured gas temperature sits at 1.4-2.2e4 K out to
r ~ 15 pc, dropping sharply to <50 K beyond r ~ 16 pc -- matching
r_HII almost exactly, and confirming the 1e4 K-order regime rather
than 47,500 K. To reproduce a config known to be in this regime:
`gas_mass={20,95}`, `nside={0,1}`, `initial_metallicity=0`,
`rebuild_time_myr=0.01`, built with the debug flag above.
